  "query_preview": "..(Wednesday through Monday)\nIssued at 532 PM EDT MON AUG 3 2026\n\nThe long wave pattern will be amplified through early next week, as \nstronger ridge centers dominate from the western Atlantic as well as \nthe Four Corners region. Meanwhile, a deeper low will be churning \nover Hudson Bay, with broad cyclonic flow fanned south across the\nnorth central CONUS. This cyclonic flow will dampen and become \nmore confined to the Upper Midwest/Great Lakes region by late this\nweekend and into early next week. Diurnally-driven convection \nwill threaten each day across eastern Kentucky, although areal \ncoverage should generally lessen by the latter half of the weekend\nand into early next week, given the weaker forcing in place and \ndecreasing PWATs across the region. \n\nWednesday will likely be the day of better areal convective \ncoverage, as a remnant upper level low weakens but also drifts \nnorth towards the Commonwealth. PWATs look to increase to the\n1.7-1.8 inch range, coupled with freezing levels above 15k feet \nagl, and storm motions of less than 10 kts. As such, isolated \nflash flooding will be possible where thunderstorms are most \npersistent. Residual troughing will then linger across the Ohio \nValley from Thursday through Saturday, before easing up more so \nSunday and Monday. Again, this should result in more limited \nconvective coverage each day, although given the successive days \nof storm potential and continued meager storm motions, isolated \nflash flooding will threaten at times through the end of the work\nweek. Highs will slowly moderate each day, starting out in the \nlow to mid 80s on Wednesday, and reaching the mid to upper 80s by \nearly next week. Lows will average in the mid to upper 60s. \nHumidity levels will remain elevated throughout the period.",
